*This question is contributed by Bright Line Watch. See also the* [*U.S. Democracy Threat Index*](https://www.metaculus.com/index/us-democracy-threat/)*.*&#x20;

For several months the [Former JAG Working Group](https://www.formerjagworkinggroup.org/) has been submitting memoranda to members of the U.S. Congress and staff members [warning](https://www.formerjagworkinggroup.org/post/sixth-memorandum-to-members-of-congress-and-staff) of possible plans to deploy troops to voting sites to intimidate voters. Federal [law](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/592) prohibits deployment of "any troops or armed men at any place where a general or special election is held, unless such force be necessary to repel armed enemies of the United States." In a recent [interview](https://time.com/article/2026/10/01/donald-trump-2026-interview-transcript/) with Time Magazine, Donald Trump replied, "I don't rule anything out or in" in response to questions about plans to declare a state of emergency in the days leading up to the election or the deployment of ICE agents to polling stations.

In October 2026 there were sightings of National Guard troops standing by an official ballot dropbox in Washington, DC, posted on social media ([example](https://x.com/YourAnonCentral/status/2108874736122171883)). However, subsequent to the social media footage, there were [reported](https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2026/10/09/troops-were-loitering-by-dc-ballot-box-by-mistake-national-guard-says/) denials that the deployment was deliberate.&#x20;

See also from The Conversation: [What the law says about armed officers at polling places](https://theconversation.com/what-the-law-says-about-armed-officers-at-polling-places-293150)
